What is NumPy?
NumPy is the fundamental package for scientific computing in Python. It is a Python library that provides a multidimensional array object, various derived objects (such as masked arrays and matrices), and an assortment of routines for fast operations on arrays, including mathematical, logical, shape manipulation, sorting, selecting, I/O, discrete Fourier transforms, basic linear algebra, basic statistical operations, random simulation and much more.

At the core of the NumPy package, is the ndarray object. This encapsulates n-dimensional arrays of homogeneous data types, with many operations being performed in compiled code for performance. There are several important differences between NumPy arrays and the standard Python sequences:

NumPy arrays have a fixed size at creation, unlike Python lists (which can grow dynamically). Changing the size of an ndarray will create a new array and delete the original.
The elements in a NumPy array are all required to be of the same data type, and thus will be the same size in memory. The exception: one can have arrays of (Python, including NumPy) objects, thereby allowing for arrays of different sized elements.
NumPy arrays facilitate advanced mathematical and other types of operations on large numbers of data. Typically, such operations are executed more efficiently and with less code than is possible using Python’s built-in sequences.
A growing plethora of scientific and mathematical Python-based packages are using NumPy arrays; though these typically support Python-sequence input, they convert such input to NumPy arrays prior to processing, and they often output NumPy arrays. In other words, in order to efficiently use much (perhaps even most) of today’s scientific/mathematical Python-based software, just knowing how to use Python’s built-in sequence types is insufficient - one also needs to know how to use NumPy arrays.
The points about sequence size and speed are particularly important in scientific computing. As a simple example, consider the case of multiplying each element in a 1-D sequence with the corresponding element in another sequence of the same length. If the data are stored in two Python lists, a and b, we could iterate over each element:

c = []
for i in range(len(a)):
c.append(a[i]*b[i])
This produces the correct answer, but if a and b each contain millions of numbers, we will pay the price for the inefficiencies of looping in Python. We could accomplish the same task much more quickly in C by writing (for clarity we neglect variable declarations and initializations, memory allocation, etc.)

for (i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
c[i] = a[i]*b[i];
}
This saves all the overhead involved in interpreting the Python code and manipulating Python objects, but at the expense of the benefits gained from coding in Python. Furthermore, the coding work required increases with the dimensionality of our data. In the case of a 2-D array, for example, the C code (abridged as before) expands to

for (i = 0; i < rows; i++) {
for (j = 0; j < columns; j++) {
c[i][j] = a[i][j]*b[i][j];
}
}
NumPy gives us the best of both worlds: element-by-element operations are the “default mode” when an ndarray is involved, but the element-by-element operation is speedily executed by pre-compiled C code. In NumPy

c = a * b
does what the earlier examples do, at near-C speeds, but with the code simplicity we expect from something based on Python. Indeed, the NumPy idiom is even simpler! This last example illustrates two of NumPy’s features which are the basis of much of its power: vectorization and broadcasting.

FastAPI API Development with Python Roadmap is a step-by-step guide focused on advancing FastAPI skills by building dynamic, validated, and performant APIs. This roadmap expands beyond basic API creation by introducing dynamic URL handling, data validation using Pydantic, and API performance analysis through response metrics visualization.

 FastAPI allows developers to create flexible REST APIs by using path parameters and query parameters. Path parameters are dynamic values included directly inside the URL, commonly used to identify specific resources.

 Example:

 @app.get("/items/{item_id}")
 def read_item(item_id: int):
  return {"item_id": item_id}

 Query parameters are optional or additional values sent through the URL to filter or customize API responses.

 Example:

 @app.get("/items/")
 def read_items(q: str | None = None):
  return {"query": q}

 FastAPI automatically validates these parameters using Python type hints, ensuring that incoming data matches the expected format.

 The roadmap also covers Data Validation with Pydantic. Pydantic allows developers to create models that define the structure and requirements of incoming data. FastAPI uses these models to validate requests automatically, generate clear error messages, and improve API reliability.

 Example:

 from pydantic import BaseModel

 class Item(BaseModel):
  name: str
  price: float

 With Pydantic models, FastAPI can validate JSON bodies, convert data types, and document API schemas automatically through OpenAPI and Swagger UI.

 Another important section is Response Metrics Visualization, which focuses on measuring API performance. Developers learn how to monitor response times, analyze request success rates, and visualize API behavior using tools like Requests, Matplotlib, and Pandas.

 This knowledge helps optimize APIs by identifying slow endpoints, performance issues, and possible improvements.

Building an Application with Spring Boot

 Spring Boot is a framework built on top of Spring Framework that simplifies the development of Java applications by reducing configuration and providing production-ready features. It allows developers to focus more on business logic and less on infrastructure setup.

 A Spring Boot application can be created quickly using Spring Initializr, which generates a preconfigured project with the required dependencies, build system, and project structure. Spring Boot applications require Java 17 or later and can be built using Maven or Gradle.

 The main advantage of Spring Boot is auto-configuration. Spring Boot analyzes the dependencies available in the project and automatically creates the required beans and configurations. It does not generate code or modify application files; instead, it dynamically configures the application context at startup.

 For example, when spring-boot-starter-webmvc is included, Spring Boot automatically configures the required Spring MVC components and an embedded servlet container such as Tomcat. If Jetty is available instead of Tomcat, Spring Boot can automatically configure Jetty. This allows developers to create web applications without manually configuring servers or infrastructure.

 The main entry point of a Spring Boot application is usually a class annotated with:

 @SpringBootApplication

 This annotation combines three important annotations:

 @Configuration: defines the class as a source of Spring bean definitions.
 @EnableAutoConfiguration: enables Spring Boot automatic configuration based on dependencies and application settings.
 @ComponentScan: searches for components, controllers, and services inside the application package.

 The application starts through:

 SpringApplication.run(Application.class, args);

 This launches the Spring application context and initializes all required beans.

 Spring Boot uses Spring MVC to create web applications and REST APIs. Controllers are commonly created using:

 @RestController

 which allows methods to handle HTTP requests and return data directly.

 Example:

 @RestController
 public class HelloController {

  @GetMapping("/")
  public String index() {
  return "Greetings from Spring Boot!";
  }
 }

 Spring Boot also simplifies testing by providing testing support through annotations such as:

 @SpringBootTest

 which loads the complete application context, and:

 @WebMvcTest

 which focuses only on the web layer.

 For production environments, Spring Boot provides Actuator, a module that adds operational endpoints for monitoring applications. Actuator provides features such as health checks, application information, metrics, and management endpoints.

 Example dependency:

 <dependency>
  <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
  <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-actuator</artifactId>
 </dependency>

 A common health check endpoint is:

 /actuator/health

 which can return the application status:

 {
  "status": "UP"
 }

 Spring Boot also simplifies application packaging by supporting executable JAR files. These JARs include the application code and embedded server, allowing the application to run directly with:

 java -jar application.jar

 Overall, Spring Boot accelerates Java backend development by providing automatic configuration, embedded servers, dependency management through starters, testing support, monitoring capabilities, and easy deployment options.

Web applications usually require additional files besides the HTML generated by the server. These files include images, CSS stylesheets, JavaScript files, and other resources needed to display a complete web page. In Django, these resources are called static files. Managing static files is simple in small projects, but it becomes more complex in larger applications where multiple apps may contain their own sets of files. Django provides the django.contrib.staticfiles application to organize these resources by collecting static files from different applications into a single location that can be served efficiently in production.

 Django searches for static files using the STATICFILES_FINDERS setting. One of the default finders is AppDirectoriesFinder, which looks for a directory named static inside each installed application. This works similarly to how Django searches for templates inside a templates directory. For example, inside the polls application, a static directory can be created at polls/static/, where Django will automatically search for CSS, images, and other resources.

 To organize static files correctly, Django recommends using namespacing. Instead of placing files directly inside polls/static/, a subdirectory with the application name should be created, such as polls/static/polls/. This prevents conflicts when different applications contain files with the same name. Django uses the first matching file it finds, so separating static files by application ensures that the correct resource is loaded.

 A stylesheet can be added by creating a file such as:

 polls/static/polls/style.css

 li a {
  color: green;
 }

 To use this stylesheet in a template, Django provides the {% static %} template tag. First, the template must load the static tag library:

 {% load static %}

 Then the stylesheet can be included using:

 <link rel="stylesheet" href="{% static 'polls/style.css' %}">

 The {% static %} tag generates the correct absolute URL for the static resource. During development, this is enough for Django to locate and serve the file automatically. After restarting the development server and loading the application, the updated styles should appear.

 Static files can also include images. For example, an image folder can be created inside the application’s static directory:

 polls/static/polls/images/background.png

 The image can then be referenced from the CSS file using a relative path:

 body {
  background: white url("images/background.png") no-repeat;
 }

 Static files should reference each other using relative paths instead of the {% static %} template tag. The reason is that CSS files are not processed by Django’s template system, so template tags are not available inside them. Using relative paths allows the value of STATIC_URL to be changed later without requiring modifications to every reference inside static files.

 The main purpose of Django’s static files system is to provide a consistent way to organize, locate, and serve resources required by web pages. By separating static resources by application, using the {% static %} template tag in templates, and keeping correct file structures, Django applications can manage CSS, images, and JavaScript files efficiently during both development and production.
